{
  "description": "The `2wai.reply` reducer: a strict in-order stream of protocol frames in, an ordered start/text/complete event log out. Derived from packages/core/src/replyStream.ts and the eleven production-fixture tests in packages/core/tests/replyStream.test.ts. The producer is clg_pp_payment `vm-mirror/wav2lip-training/home/azureuser/livealman-host/brain_llm.py` (origin/staging) `BrainStream._emit_reply`, which emits exactly {v, reply_id, seq, kind, text?} with kind in start|delta|end|cancel|error, seq starting at 0 on the start frame, and the terminal frame carrying the full accumulated text as its authoritative value. Four rules are decisions rather than mechanics: (1) a frame is accepted only if its reply_id is the active one AND its seq is exactly the next expected one, so a duplicate, a stale reply, and an out-of-order delta all drop; (2) a terminal frame's own text REPLACES the accumulated buffer, which is how a missed delta repairs itself; (3) Voice Expression tags are stripped at one chokepoint (the accumulate step), so the buffer, the text events and the completion are clean by construction and a future callback cannot forget to strip — the vocabulary is EXACT, never a greedy bracket sweep, because a greedy sweep eats [CARD: ...] chips and [email] PII placeholders; (4) `sawProtocol` LATCHES on any well-formed frame, including one the gate then drops, and it is what makes the protocol supersede fallback transcription (packages/core/src/engine.ts, consumeFallbackTranscription). THE SPLIT-TAG CONTRACT, because it is the one thing here that looks like a bug and is not. Stripping happens per CHUNK, so a tag split across two deltas (\"[fli\" then \"rty] hi\") matches neither chunk and leaks into the text events until the reply completes. That is deliberate, and it rests on three things: (a) the PRODUCER guarantee — clg_pp_payment `livealman-host/brain_llm.py` `_run()` always emits the terminal frame with `text=\"\".join(self._reply_text)`, the whole reply, on every exit path (end, cancel, error); (b) tags not spanning deltas is a producer property, not something a consumer can enforce — the brain emits a tag inside one SSE delta; (c) the terminal frame's authoritative text is therefore the BACKSTOP: it is stripped as a whole, so the split tag is repaired at completion and the persisted/final copy is always clean. A reducer that buffered instead, to strip only across a complete reply, would trade a transient flicker for waiting on the whole reply before painting anything — the exact latency the delta stream exists to avoid. The scenario below encodes the OBSERVABLE behaviour so a Dart port reproduces it rather than \"fixing\" it into a divergence. One constraint that cannot be commented in the frozen source: the tag vocabulary is interpolated straight into a RegExp alternation, so every entry must stay free of regex metacharacters. A tag containing `(`, `.`, `[`, `+` or `?` would silently change what the pattern matches, or throw at module load. Spaces are fine (\"low voice\", \"breathes deeply\"). Scenario shape: `frames` is replayed in order. Two entries are directives rather than frames: `{\"op\": \"reset\"}` is the reset a new outbound turn performs, and `{\"op\": \"keepTags\", \"value\": <bool>}` flips the admin-review flag MID-STREAM (it is a live closure, read on every strip, not a value snapshotted at construction). `expectEvents` is the exact ordered event log. `expectAccepted` (optional) pins the per-entry accept/reject verdict, with null in the slot of a directive. `keepTags` sets the starting value and DEFAULTS TO false; it is display only, and the only thing it changes is whether direction tags survive into the text.",
